Q: Converting from an integer to its binary representation A: <p><strong>Golang - convert [8]bool to byte or uint8</strong>
<em>(8 packed bits into a byte)</em></p>
<p>This solution is probably clearer, more straighforward to understand, beginner friendly.</p>
<pre><code>    transform  flags into actual bits:
    [ true true false false]  -&gt; [ high high low low] -&gt; 1100 -&gt; 0b1100 -&gt; deciaml: 12
    [ true false false true]  -&gt; [ high low low high] -&gt; 1001 -&gt; 0b1001 -&gt; decimal:  9 
</code></pre>
<p><strong>playground link, runnable code</strong>:</p>
<p><a href="https://go.dev/play/p/ZirYdWtjfcV" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://go.dev/play/p/ZirYdWtjfcV</a></p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>import &quot;fmt&quot;



func main() {

    // Bool8ToUint8 - sums only a few predefined weights
    Bool8ToUint8 := func(mei [8]bool) uint8 {
        bits := [8]uint8{0b10000000, 0b01000000, 0b00100000, 0b00010000, 0b00010000, 0b00000100, 0b00000010, 0b00000001}

        var result uint8
        for i, b := range mei {
            if b {
                result += bits[i] // operates only on the '1' bits
            }
        }
        return result
    }

    // usage:
    mei := [8]bool{true, true, false, false, false, false, false, false}
    myvar := Bool8ToUint8(mei)
    fmt.Printf(&quot;%d == 0b%08b\n&quot;, myvar, myvar) // 192 == 0b11000000

    mei = [8]bool{false, false, false, false, false, true, false, true}
    myvar = Bool8ToUint8(mei)
    fmt.Printf(&quot;%d == 0b%08b\n&quot;, myvar, myvar) // 5 == 0b00000101
}


</code></pre>

Q: Android: Robolectric test never finish, runs forever (MAC m2 apple silicon) A: <p>This can also be an architecture mismatch specific to Apple Silicon, worth ruling out alongside the download-timing angle.</p>
<p>Robolectric's native runtime needs to resolve a native library classifier matching your OS and CPU architecture. On M2, that means it needs the <code>mac-aarch64</code> artifact specifically, not the older <code>mac-x86_64</code> one. If something in the resolution chain (proxy, VPN, or a stale Gradle cache) can't reach or resolve the correct arm64 classifier, Robolectric doesn't always fail with a clear error, it can sit retrying or waiting on a socket that never returns, which looks like &quot;runs forever.&quot;</p>
<p>A few things worth checking, in order:</p>
<p><strong>1. Confirm your JDK architecture:</strong></p>
<pre><code>java -version
</code></pre>
<p>Check the output specifies <code>aarch64</code>, not <code>x86_64</code>. Running an x86_64 JDK under Rosetta on an M2 Mac is a common, easy-to-miss cause of exactly this kind of native library mismatch.</p>
<p><strong>2. Check whether it's stuck on network resolution vs. native loading:</strong></p>
<pre><code>./gradlew test --info
</code></pre>
<p>Watch whether it stalls on a Maven Central lookup or stalls after dependencies are already resolved. That tells you which half of the problem you're dealing with.</p>
<p><strong>3. Clear the dependency cache and let it re-resolve fresh:</strong></p>
<pre><code>rm -rf ~/.m2/repository/org/robolectric
</code></pre>
<p>A partially-downloaded or corrupted native artifact from an earlier interrupted run can cause silent hangs on retry.</p>
<p><strong>4. If it's still stuck</strong>, force explicit resolution instead of relying on a possibly stale cached artifact:</p>
<pre><code>android {
    testOptions {
        unitTests {
            all {
                test -&gt; test.systemProperties[&quot;robolectric.offline&quot;] = &quot;false&quot;
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Sharing what <code>java -version</code> reports and whether <code>--info</code> shows it stuck on resolution vs. native loading would narrow this down to one specific cause.</p>
